  "Number": 6,
  "Question": "Real numbers \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) satisfy the equation \\(x^2 + y^2 = 10x - 6y - 34.\\) What is \\(x+y?\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(1\\)",
    "B": "\\(2\\)",
    "C": "\\(3\\)",
    "D": "\\(6\\)",
    "E": "\\(8\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "Completing the square is useful because real squares cannot be negative. Rearranging the equation and using \\(25+9=34\\), we obtain\n\\[\n0=x^2-10x+y^2+6y+34\n=(x-5)^2+(y+3)^2.\n\\]\nBoth terms are nonnegative, so their sum can be zero only when both are zero. Therefore \\(x=5\\) and \\(y=-3\\). This pair makes both squares zero and hence satisfies the original equation. Thus\n\\[\nx+y=5-3=2,\n\\]\nwhich is choice B.",
